From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 08:55:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Run the redstone power queries on coordinates

isBlockIndirectlyPowered built a BlockPosition per direction, and each of those could
reach getBlockPower, which built six more - up to 42 positions for one query. Hoppers,
doors, dispensers, TNT, rails, note blocks and redstone lamps all run this on their own
tick.

The coordinate arithmetic now stays in ints, and a position is materialized only where the
Block API actually consumes one. hasRedstonePowerHandler() narrows that to the eleven
classes that override getWeakPower or getStrongPower; every other block inherits the
constant 0, so building a position to ask it was waste. The flag is a plain "does this
class override it" question rather than a reuse of isPowerSource(), which is state
dependent on BlockChest and BlockRedstoneWire and so is not the same question.

A shared scratch position is deliberately not used here: getWeakPower and getStrongPower
can re-enter these methods, as redstone wire does, so the nesting depth is unbounded and a
fixed slot would be scribbled over mid-query.

Measured on a live server, the elided call fires about a thousand times a second while a
redstone circuit runs.

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/Block.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/Block.java
index 5afbf410b2dd13f1e43a56c076959f1d3b0b456a..5f82e68acd65daf05cc4dd3a907bc35bb22cf146 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/Block.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/Block.java
@@ -628,6 +628,18 @@ public class Block {
         return 0;
     }
 
+    // Athena - true when this block overrides getWeakPower or getStrongPower.
+    //
+    // World's redstone power queries build a BlockPosition purely to hand it to those two methods.
+    // Every other block inherits the constant 0 above, so for them the position is waste - and the
+    // queries walk all six neighbours of a block, recursing through occluding ones. This is a plain
+    // "does this class override it" flag rather than a reuse of isPowerSource(), because
+    // isPowerSource() is state-dependent on BlockChest and BlockRedstoneWire and so is not the same
+    // question. Declaring either override implies this, and subclasses inherit it.
+    public boolean hasRedstonePowerHandler() {
+        return false;
+    }
+
     public void setBlockBoundsForItemRender() {}
 
     public void harvestBlock(World world, EntityHuman entityhuman, BlockPosition blockposition, IBlockData iblockdata, TileEntity tileentity) {
